## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). Technology-facilitated violence is part of the **contemporary layer** — the 2026 paper asked about the digital gender gap. Define it, give its **forms**, Pakistani **cases**, the **law** (PECA 2016) and a short assessment with remedies.

## The model answer

### 1. Definition and context

#### i) Definition
**Technology-facilitated gender-based violence** is violence against women carried out or amplified through phones, the internet and social media — harassment, stalking, threats and abuse that cause psychological, social and sometimes physical harm.

#### ii) The context
More Pakistani women are online than ever: the **mobile-internet gender gap** fell from **38%** to **25%** (GSMA, ==2024–25==), bringing about **eight million** more women online. Digital space offers voice, work and learning — and new forms of control.

#### iii) Why it is gender-based
It targets women **as women**: sexualised abuse and threats to family "honour" aim to push women out of public space — extending **Liz Kelly**'s **continuum of violence** (==1988==) into the digital world.

### 2. Forms

#### i) Harassment and threats
Abusive messages, sexual comments and threats, often from strangers and sometimes coordinated against outspoken women.

#### ii) Blackmail and image-based abuse
Threats to share **private images** — real or doctored — to extort money, sex or obedience; fake profiles that damage a woman's reputation.

#### iii) Stalking and doxxing
Tracking a woman's location and publishing her **private details** (doxxing), exposing her to offline danger.

#### iv) Control within the family
Husbands and relatives monitoring phones, restricting women's access to the internet, or using online activity as "proof" of dishonour.

::: fig cycle | How online violence becomes offline harm.
@centre A woman's online presence
Harassment and threats
Blackmail with private images
Doxxing exposes her location
Family "honour" reaction
Withdrawal from online life — or worse
:::

### 3. Cases

#### i) Qandeel Baloch (2016)
The social-media figure was strangled by her brother in **Multan** in **July 2016**, who claimed her online videos dishonoured the family — the case that led to the **October 2016** honour-killing law.

#### ii) Sana Yousaf (2025)
The 17-year-old social-media creator was shot dead in Islamabad in **June 2025** after refusing a man's advances.

#### iii) Women in public life
Women journalists, politicians and activists face trolling, doctored images and sexual slurs; marchers in the **Aurat March** faced online abuse and threats.

### 4. The legal and institutional response

#### i) PECA 2016
The **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act 2016** criminalises offences against the **dignity and modesty** of a person (sections 20–21) and **cyber-stalking** (section 24).

#### ii) The Punjab Act 2016
The **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016** lists **cybercrime** and **stalking** among the forms of violence in section 2(r).

#### iii) Helplines
The **Digital Rights Foundation** has run a **cyber-harassment helpline** since ==2016==.

### 5. Why it persists

#### i) Shame and silence
Victims fear that reporting will expose the images or messages to their families, so many stay silent — the same silence that leaves **56%** of women who suffer violence never seeking help (PDHS ==2017–18==).

#### ii) Weak enforcement
Investigation capacity is limited, cases are slow and conviction is rare.

#### iii) Platforms and anonymity
Anonymous accounts and slow platform responses make abusers hard to identify and content hard to remove.

#### iv) Victim-blaming
Families and even police often blame the woman for being online at all — asking why she posted photos — rather than the abuser.

#### v) The digital divide in knowledge
Many women, especially new users, lack digital-safety skills.

### 6. Remedies

#### i) Enforcement
Specialised, well-staffed cyber-crime units with **women officers**, fast removal of abusive content and time-bound investigation.

#### ii) Prevention
**Digital-safety education** in schools and for new women users; campaigns against victim-blaming.

#### iii) Support
Expand helplines, legal aid and counselling; link online abuse to protection orders under the provincial violence laws.

#### iv) Platforms
Require platforms to respond quickly to complaints in Urdu and regional languages.

#### v) The line to take
Digital space is both a **site of feminist voice** and a **new arena of patriarchal control**; closing the mobile gap must go together with making the internet safe for women.

## Why this answer scores
- A clear **definition** set in the context of the **narrowing mobile gap** (38% to 25%).
- Organises **forms** into four headings and gives Pakistani **cases** (Qandeel Baloch, Sana Yousaf).
- Names the **law** precisely (PECA 2016, sections 20–21 and 24; the Punjab Act 2016).
- Explains **why it persists** and offers practical **remedies**; a **cycle** figure shows online harm becoming offline harm.
